      "lineNbr": 131,
      "author": {
        "id": 1072905
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-12-01T16:35:22Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The reason we use `sorted()` rather than `min()` is to get deterministic tie-breaking when multiple enforcers report the same number of available tokens.\n\nThis concern was brought up by @ponch78@gmail.com here [1].\n\n`Stream.sorted()` is stable, so in the case of a tie it preserves the original order of `responses()`.\n\nI agree the current comment doesn\u0027t make that clear, I\u0027ll rephrase it to explain the tie-breaking requirement rather than just stating \"sorted is stable\".\n\nThe cost of sorting is negligible here because the number of enforcers is very small, but the deterministic behaviour avoids surprising results.\n\n[1] https://gerrit-review.googlesource.com/c/gerrit/+/530501/comment/261736c2_aae77ab3/",
      "parentUuid": "46e9e8af_162a2ad8",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 131,
        "startChar": 47,
        "endLine": 131,
        "endChar": 83
      },
      "revId": "2a1a6bea4f6dcf3e9e18e307f21fc8b491fabff6",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
